# One line-ending rule for the whole repository: store LF, check out LF. Git normalises on commit,
# so a Windows editor that writes CRLF still produces a clean diff against CI, and a contributor on
# any OS sees the same bytes. Without this, every commit from Windows rewrites whole files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary containers. `binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff -merge`:
#   -text    never convert line endings. A CRLF rewrite inside a zip-based file corrupts it beyond
#            repair, and git would not notice because the damage is inside the compressed stream.
#   -diff    never attempt a textual diff. On a zip the output is either "Binary files differ" or,
#            worse, a wall of noise that hides the real change.
#   -merge   never attempt a three-way merge. Two people editing one binary is a choice, not a patch:
#            take one side, then redo the edit.
#
# Git guesses binary-ness by looking for NUL bytes and would usually get these right on its own.
# Declaring them removes the guess, which still matters for a file added with `git add --force`.
#
# Documents are deliberately absent from the repository (*.docx in .gitignore), but the attributes
# stay: a forced add must not be able to corrupt one.
*.docx binary
*.doc binary
*.xlsx binary
*.xls binary
*.pptx binary
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary
*.woff binary
